So, we will now work on pairSum. PairSum is like, we will be given a certain target. And from
the array, we will have to find the pair whose summation will be equal to the target. This is 
the main goal of this problem. And to solve this problem, we have  
1. Brute-force way
2. efficient way 


1. Brute-force way:
In the brute force way, that is in the most in-efficient way, we will extract every pair from the loop
and then, add the pair and see if it matches the target or not. if it does, we will put the elements of
the array into a vector and push back and then later print it. Time complexity of this brute force method
is O(n^2)   

2. Efficient way:
In the efficient way, the time complexity will be O(n). Here, we will traverse the entire array once
we will use two pointer. One pointer at the first element of the loop (indicating the smallest element 
since the array will be sorted) and the another pointer at the last element of the loop(indicating 
the largest element since the array will be sorted). Then we will add the two elemements and will find
the pairSum. Now, three casses might appear:
   i) pairSum>target----> j--
   ii) pairSum<target--->i++
   iii)pairSum==target

  Here, 'i' is set as the first element of the array while 'j' is set as the last element of the array
  Now, while(i<j), 'i' and 'j' will be added, and then compared. If, the i) occurs, j--. If, the ii) occurs, i++
  and if iii) occurs, then push the value of i and j into the vector and then later print it

  [1,2,3,4,5], i=1 and j=5 and lets say target is = 5. 
  So, at first, (i+j)==6>5(target), so, j--
  So, now, j=4, Therefore, (i+j)==5=5(target), so, when i=1 and j=4, that is in the index
  0 and 3, the target will be found, and hence the output will be [0,3];  

   
    


